      "writtenOn": "2026-08-19T07:50:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the pointer to 36517 — that\u0027s really helpful context.\n\nAfter reviewing it, it looks like there are two related-but-distinct sub-languages in Entity Manager:\n\n- Template / name expressions — arithmetic and $ variable substitution (e.g., \"PowerSupply$ADDRESS % 80\"), which 36517 tackles using boost::spirit::x3 with operator precedence.\n- Probe expressions — the boolean language (AND / OR / FOUND / D-Bus probes) where this bug lives. 36517 doesn\u0027t cover this grammar.\n\nThe probe grammar I wrote is meant to complement 36517 rather than overlap with it. Since 36517 is still active, I\u0027d rather align with your direction there than build something separate — so before writing any parser code, I\u0027d appreciate your guidance:\n\n1. Tooling: For the probe parser, would you prefer boost::spirit::x3 (to stay consistent with 36517), or is a small hand-written lexer/parser acceptable for this grammar?\n2. Scope: Should this remain probe-focused, or eventually converge with the ongoing general expression parser from 36517 into a single framework?\n3. Incremental approach: Would a lexer-only change — retrofitting the existing probe machinery to operate on tokens instead of raw strings — be a welcome first step? It fixes the original substring-matching bug with minimal code changes.\n\nMatt Spinler/ Ed Tanous — please let me know your preferred direction whenever you have a moment. I\u0027m happy to align with the 36517 approach or whichever path you feel is best.",
